      <slug></slug>
      <title>LanguageKit - supporting Smalltalk and JavaScript dialects on the Objective-C runtime - what&#39;s hard, what&#39;s easy, and why developers and users should care.</title>
      <subtitle></subtitle>
      <track>World of GNUstep devroom</track>
      <type></type>
      <language></language>
      <abstract>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The GNUstep Objective-C runtime was designed to make it relatively easy to support other dynamic languages, sharing an object model with Objective-C. This talk will discuss some of the features and how they are used by LanguageKit, a framework designed to make it easy to implement domain-specific languages with static compilation, JIT and interpreter modes.&lt;/p&gt;      </abstract>
      <description>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The GNUstep Objective-C runtime was designed to make it relatively easy to support other dynamic languages, sharing an object model with Objective-C. This talk will discuss some of the features and how they are used by LanguageKit, a framework designed to make it easy to implement domain-specific languages with static compilation, JIT and interpreter modes.&lt;/p&gt;      </description>
      <persons>
          <person id="961">David Chisnall</person>
      </persons>
